WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.750
One of my students said her leaf model

2
00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:05.770
was perfect: twenty out of twenty!

3
00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.640
I asked which photos she’d tested it on.

4
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.800
The same twenty she’d trained it on.

5
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.590
Today: how to test a model honestly,

6
00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:16.890
and what its mistakes tell you.

7
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:20.280
Before you train, put about one

8
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.500
in five examples aside.

9
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.510
The model never sees them while it learns.

10
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.670
They’re your test set, the only

11
00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:28.850
fair way to measure it.

12
00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:30.890
Then test.

13
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.200
Show the model each test example and

14
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:36.420
compare its answer with the real label.

15
00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:37.900
Count the right ones:

16
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.920
seventeen out of twenty, say.

17
00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.210
That’s fair, because they were new to it.

18
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.900
Now look at the three wrong

19
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.860
ones: there are two kinds.

20
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.240
A false positive says yes when it’s

21
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.320
no: “sick” for a healthy leaf.

22
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:54.910
That’s a false alarm.

23
00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.380
A false negative says no when it’s

24
00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.760
yes: “healthy” for a sick leaf.

25
00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:02.190
That’s a miss.

26
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.520
Why not test on the training examples?

27
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.340
Because a model can memorise them.

28
00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:10.710
Overfitting is when a model is

29
00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.050
perfect on its training

30
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.210
examples, but poor on new ones.

31
00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.790
It memorised, instead of

32
00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:17.510
learning the pattern.

33
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.170
When I moved to a new town, I

34
00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.830
found my way to school by

35
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.310
landmarks: left at the red kiosk.

36
00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.910
Then someone painted the kiosk

37
00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.830
blue, and I was lost!

38
00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:34.760
My neighbour could read the map, so

39
00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.860
she found the way from anywhere.

40
00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:39.760
Landmarks are memorising;

41
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:41.660
the map is learning.

42
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:43.430
Quick check!

43
00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.480
A model gets twenty out of twenty on its

44
00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:51.280
training photos, but eleven on new ones.

45
00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.110
What’s going on?

46
00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.340
It memorised: overfitting.

47
00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:59.290
A big gap between the training and

48
00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.290
test scores is the warning sign.

49
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.120
More, and more varied, examples help.

50
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:07.490
So does a simpler model that

51
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:08.670
can’t just memorise.

52
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.360
Which mistake matters more

53
00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:12.640
depends on your model.

54
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.070
For a leaf-disease spotter, a miss is

55
00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.430
worse: one sick plant can infect the

56
00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:19.790
field.

57
00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.730
For a spam filter, a false alarm is worse:

58
00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.190
a message from school gets hidden.

59
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.890
In the app, circles are the training

60
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.670
examples and squares are new ones.

61
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:36.500
Give the model more freedom, a bendier

62
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.120
curve, and watch it hit every circle

63
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:40.320
while missing the squares.

64
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.800
Switch to forty examples,

65
00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:44.300
and it calms down.

66
00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:46.230
Your turn.

67
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.970
Your model spots malaria in

68
00:02:48.970 --> 00:02:50.390
microscope photos of blood.

69
00:02:51.020 --> 00:02:52.780
Which mistake matters more?

70
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.700
Missing a real case: a sick person

71
00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.000
could go untreated, while a false

72
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.860
alarm means a second test.

73
00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:04.550
Remember: keep a test set apart, and

74
00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:06.210
count right and wrong on it.

75
00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.170
Perfect on training, poor on new

76
00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:11.390
examples: that’s overfitting.

77
00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:14.700
And decide which mistake costs more.

78
00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.330
Try it yourself: ask a friend to

79
00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.390
memorise the answers to five sums.

80
00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:22.670
Then give them five new

81
00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:24.030
sums of the same kind.

82
00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:27.090
If they only memorised, they’ll struggle.

83
00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:29.680
Then explain overfitting,

84
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:30.980
using what just happened.
